;; The agent actor that translates published documents.
;;
;; Composed rather than invented. `contracts/roles/translator.edn` already
;; declares the role, and `contracts/capabilities/cap_translation.edn` already
;; grants the one tool this work needs — `save_translation`, whose own
;; description says "Humans review and approve translations in the translation
;; workspace; agents use save_translation." This contract is the missing third
;; piece: an agent that holds that role.
;;
;; It declares no trigger, no schedule and no event filter, per
;; `docs/design/resource-architecture.md`: "Agents are prompt/model/capability
;; specs — they are not triggers, actions, or schedules." The trigger that runs
;; it lives in `contracts/namespaces/publication.edn`.
;;
;; The document, garden, locale and dispatch claim are NOT named here. They are
;; per-attempt, and they arrive as the resource-policy overlay
;; `law.translation-agent/session-policies` builds — see that namespace for why
;; the pin travels on the event rather than in a contract.
{:contract/id "publication_translator"
 :contract/kind :agent
 :contract/actors ["pi"]
 :enabled true

 ;; No passive memory hydration and no sources. The translation input is handed
 ;; to the session on the event, in full, at the exact revision the claim was
 ;; taken for. Hydrating prior sessions in would let context from a *different*
 ;; revision of the same document leak into this translation, which is the one
 ;; kind of contamination a revision-pinned receipt cannot detect.
 :context {:max-messages 12
           :max-chars 400000
           :preserve-system true}

 ;; Exactly one tool, and it is load-bearing for the OpenPlanner cutover rather
 ;; than a tidiness preference.
 ;;
 ;; `:role/translator` also grants :cap/read, :cap/memory and :cap/semantic,
 ;; which exist for the *human* translation workspace. :cap/semantic carries
 ;; `graph_query`, and `graph_query` is one of the ten operations
 ;; `infra.clients.openplanner-mongo` still delegates to OpenPlanner REST — so a
 ;; translation session was reaching `POST /v1/graph/memory` on every turn.
 ;; Observed: fourteen such calls, every one a 500, on a stack whose whole point
 ;; is that OpenPlanner is gone. Where the deployment is configured correctly
 ;; those become "OpenPlanner is not configured" instead, which is a hard tool
 ;; error rather than a hang, but still not translation.
 ;;
 ;; The session needs nothing else: the source bytes arrive on the event, the
 ;; document/garden/locale arrive as the resource-policy pin, and the only
 ;; lawful output is one submitted pair for each server-issued split.
 ;;
 ;; This key was inert on agent contracts until `domain.contracts.resolve`
 ;; learned to read it — see `allowed-tool-ids` there.
 :tools/allowed ["save_translation"]
 :tools/choice :required-first

 :agent
 {:roles [:role/translator]
  :model "gemma4:31b"
  :thinking :medium}

 :prompts
 {:system
  "You translate one document into one target language, and you do nothing else.

The task message contains the complete source document and names the target
language. Translate that text — not a summary of it, not a commentary on it.

Rules:

- Preserve the Markdown structure exactly: heading levels, paragraph breaks,
  list markers, emphasis, link targets, and code blocks. Translate link text,
  never link URLs. Never translate the contents of a code block.
- Preserve the paragraph count. The publication renderer splits on blank lines,
  so merging or splitting paragraphs changes the rendered document.
- Do not translate proper nouns that are product, project, or organization
  names. \"Open Hax\", \"Knoxx\", and \"Promethean\" stay as written.
- Do not add a translator's note, a preface, a language label, or any text that
  is not a translation of the source.

For every server-issued split, call `save_translation` exactly once. The call
MUST include the non-empty translated result in the required `translated_text`
field and the supplied `split_id`, `attempt_id`, and `segment_index`. For
example, the arguments have this shape:
`{\"translated_text\":\"<translation only>\",\"split_id\":\"<exact id>\",
\"attempt_id\":\"<exact id>\",\"segment_index\":0}`. Do not invent, merge,
renumber, or omit split boundaries. Do not send `source_text`, `document_id`,
`garden_id`, `source_lang`, or `target_lang` — the server binds those
authoritative values from the admitted split and session.

If `save_translation` reports a validation or policy error, correct the
arguments and call it again. Never finish the turn with an unaccepted split.

The reply names the revision a human reviewer will approve. Nothing you submit
is published until a person approves that exact revision, so submit the best
translation you can rather than a partial one you intend to refine. The exact
set of submissions becomes one reviewable candidate revision; corrections are
immutable review evidence over its individual splits."}}
